Rem ==================================================================
Rem seedvt100.shoddy — reckoner seed: vt100.shoddy, escape sequences
Rem as strings
Rem
Rem THIS SEED EXISTS BECAUSE A NOTE WAS WRONG. halifax-core.shoddy
Rem listed vt100.shoddy among the machines a prompt could not reach,
Rem under "the terminal, which this shell already owns. Two things
Rem driving one screen is one thing driving it wrongly." That is a true
Rem sentence about a machine that DRIVES a terminal, and vt100.shoddy is
Rem not one. Its own header says so: "Pure — nothing here writes to the
Rem screen." Every word below EvalKey BUILDS AND RETURNS A STRING. The
Rem program does the printing, or does not.
Rem
Rem So the note confused building an escape sequence with emitting one.
Rem There was never a rule in the way, and nothing here needs the
Rem resource table, a guarded builtin or a capability: this is the most
Rem ordinary kind of seed there is, a machine of pure functions over
Rem STRINGs and NUMBERs.
Rem
Rem WHAT A SESSION DOES WITH THE STRING is its own business, and there
Rem are two honest answers. It can join sequences with + and WRITEFILE
Rem the result, which is how you build a banner, a terminal script or a
Rem fixture. And halifax PRINTS what a line answers — so a string of
Rem escapes will reach the terminal and take effect.
Rem
Rem THAT SECOND ONE IS WORTH SAYING PLAINLY: a session can scramble its
Rem own display this way, and CLEARSCREEN will do exactly what it says.
Rem That is not a leak and not a rule broken — it is the operator's own
Rem terminal, doing what the operator asked, exactly as if they had
Rem typed the escape by hand. The stack, the dictionary and the tape are
Rem untouched, and CLEAR or a redraw puts the screen back.
Rem
Rem THE CONSTANTS ARE REGISTERED FROM A TABLE, not one RckReg line
Rem apiece. Eighty-nine words that differ only in a name, a string and a
Rem sentence are data, and writing them as data is what lets the list be
Rem read down and checked against the VT100 reference in one pass. The
Rem eight that take numbers and the one that reads a key are registered
Rem by hand below, because each has a guard of its own.
Rem
Rem ESC AND CSI ARE BRIDGED TOO, deliberately. They are the building
Rem blocks the rest is made of, and with them a session can assemble a
Rem sequence this machine never named — which is better than a bridge
Rem that only reaches the ninety-eight somebody thought of.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "vt100.shoddy"
Include "cuttle.shoddy"
Include "reckoner.shoddy"
Include "seq.shoddy"

Rem ---- a constant word, as data -----------------------------------------

Type VtWord
    VtName As String
    VtText As String
    VtDesc As String

Def VsRegOne(st As RckState, v As VtWord) As RckState
    RckReg(st, VtName(v), 0, RckPure(Fn(vs) => RckVals({ CutStr(VtText(v)) })), "( -- str )", VtDesc(v))

Def VsRegAll(st As RckState, ws As List Of VtWord) As RckState
    Fold(ws, st, VsRegOne)

Rem ---- the constants, in the reference's own order -----------------------

Rem Split across several Defs only because one list literal of the whole
Rem table is a wall nobody can read; the grouping follows vt100.shoddy's
Rem own section headings.

Def VsBuilding() As List Of VtWord
    { VtWord("VTESC", Esc(), "The bare escape character. With VTCSI, the building block every other sequence here is made of — join them with + to assemble one this machine does not name."),
      VtWord("VTCSI", Csi(), "The Control Sequence Introducer, escape and [ — the opening of most VT100 sequences.") }

Def VsModes() As List Of VtWord
    { VtWord("VTNEWLINEMODE", SetNewlineMode(), "Set newline mode: a Return sends both carriage return and line feed."),
      VtWord("VTLINEFEEDMODE", SetLinefeedMode(), "Set line feed mode: a Return sends carriage return alone."),
      VtWord("VTCURSORKEYAPP", SetCursorKeyApp(), "Cursor keys send application-mode sequences, which VTEVALKEY tells apart from the normal ones."),
      VtWord("VTCURSORKEYNORMAL", SetCursorKeyNormal(), "Cursor keys send the normal-mode sequences."),
      VtWord("VT132COLS", Set132Cols(), "Switch the screen to 132 columns."),
      VtWord("VT80COLS", Set80Cols(), "Switch the screen back to 80 columns."),
      VtWord("VTSMOOTHSCROLL", SetSmoothScroll(), "Scroll smoothly rather than a whole line at a time."),
      VtWord("VTJUMPSCROLL", SetJumpScroll(), "Scroll a whole line at a time."),
      VtWord("VTREVERSESCREEN", SetReverseScreen(), "Dark characters on a light screen."),
      VtWord("VTNORMALSCREEN", SetNormalScreen(), "Light characters on a dark screen, the usual way round."),
      VtWord("VTORIGINRELATIVE", SetOriginRelative(), "Cursor positions are counted from the top of the scrolling region."),
      VtWord("VTORIGINABSOLUTE", SetOriginAbsolute(), "Cursor positions are counted from the top of the screen."),
      VtWord("VTAUTOWRAP", SetAutoWrap(), "Text past the right margin wraps to the next line."),
      VtWord("VTNOAUTOWRAP", ResetAutoWrap(), "Text past the right margin overwrites the last column instead of wrapping."),
      VtWord("VTAUTOREPEAT", SetAutoRepeat(), "A held key repeats."),
      VtWord("VTNOAUTOREPEAT", ResetAutoRepeat(), "A held key does not repeat."),
      VtWord("VTINTERLACE", SetInterlace(), "Interlaced scan."),
      VtWord("VTNOINTERLACE", ResetInterlace(), "Non-interlaced scan."),
      VtWord("VTVT52MODE", SetVt52Mode(), "Leave ANSI mode for VT52 compatibility mode, where the VT52 words below apply."),
      VtWord("VTANSIMODE", SetAnsiMode(), "Return to ANSI mode from VT52 mode.") }

Def VsKeypad() As List Of VtWord
    { VtWord("VTALTKEYPAD", SetAltKeypad(), "Application keypad mode, which is what makes a keypad key tellable from the same digit on the top row."),
      VtWord("VTNUMKEYPAD", SetNumKeypad(), "Numeric keypad mode, where a keypad digit sends the same byte as the main keyboard's.") }

Def VsCharsets() As List Of VtWord
    { VtWord("VTUKG0", SetUkG0(), "The UK character set as G0."),
      VtWord("VTUKG1", SetUkG1(), "The UK character set as G1."),
      VtWord("VTUSG0", SetUsG0(), "The US ASCII character set as G0."),
      VtWord("VTUSG1", SetUsG1(), "The US ASCII character set as G1."),
      VtWord("VTSPECIALG0", SetSpecialG0(), "The special graphics set as G0 — the line-drawing characters."),
      VtWord("VTSPECIALG1", SetSpecialG1(), "The special graphics set as G1."),
      VtWord("VTALTROMG0", SetAltRomG0(), "The alternate character ROM as G0."),
      VtWord("VTALTROMG1", SetAltRomG1(), "The alternate character ROM as G1."),
      VtWord("VTALTROMSPECIALG0", SetAltRomSpecialG0(), "The alternate ROM's special graphics as G0."),
      VtWord("VTALTROMSPECIALG1", SetAltRomSpecialG1(), "The alternate ROM's special graphics as G1."),
      VtWord("VTSHIFT2", SetShift2(), "Shift out: use G1 for the characters that follow."),
      VtWord("VTSHIFT3", SetShift3(), "Shift in: use G0 for the characters that follow.") }

Def VsAttrs() As List Of VtWord
    { VtWord("VTATTRSOFF", AttrsOff(), "Turn every character attribute off — the one to end a sequence with, so what follows is plain."),
      VtWord("VTBOLD", Bold(), "Bold, or increased intensity."),
      VtWord("VTLOWINTENSITY", LowIntensity(), "Dim, or decreased intensity."),
      VtWord("VTUNDERLINE", Underline(), "Underlined."),
      VtWord("VTBLINK", Blink(), "Blinking."),
      VtWord("VTREVERSEVIDEO", ReverseVideo(), "Foreground and background swapped."),
      VtWord("VTINVISIBLE", Invisible(), "Invisible — written but not shown.") }

Def VsCursor() As List Of VtWord
    { VtWord("VTCURSORHOME", CursorHome(), "Put the cursor at the top left."),
      VtWord("VTHVHOME", HvHome(), "Horizontal and vertical home — the top left, by the other sequence that means it."),
      VtWord("VTINDEXDOWN", IndexDown(), "Move down one line, scrolling if at the bottom."),
      VtWord("VTREVERSEINDEX", ReverseIndex(), "Move up one line, scrolling if at the top."),
      VtWord("VTNEXTLINE", NextLine(), "Move to the start of the next line."),
      VtWord("VTSAVECURSOR", SaveCursor(), "Remember where the cursor is."),
      VtWord("VTRESTORECURSOR", RestoreCursor(), "Put the cursor back where VTSAVECURSOR left it.") }

Def VsTabs() As List Of VtWord
    { VtWord("VTTABSET", TabSet(), "Set a tab stop at the cursor's column."),
      VtWord("VTTABCLEAR", TabClear(), "Clear the tab stop at the cursor's column."),
      VtWord("VTTABCLEARALL", TabClearAll(), "Clear every tab stop.") }

Def VsLines() As List Of VtWord
    { VtWord("VTDOUBLEHEIGHTTOP", DoubleHeightTop(), "This line is the TOP half of a double-height line."),
      VtWord("VTDOUBLEHEIGHTBOTTOM", DoubleHeightBottom(), "This line is the BOTTOM half of a double-height line — both halves carry the same text."),
      VtWord("VTSINGLEWIDTHHEIGHT", SingleWidthHeight(), "This line is ordinary single width and height."),
      VtWord("VTDOUBLEWIDTHHEIGHT", DoubleWidthHeight(), "This line is double width, single height.") }

Def VsErasing() As List Of VtWord
    { VtWord("VTCLEAREOL", ClearEol(), "Erase from the cursor to the end of the line."),
      VtWord("VTCLEARBOL", ClearBol(), "Erase from the start of the line to the cursor."),
      VtWord("VTCLEARLINE", ClearLine(), "Erase the whole line the cursor is on."),
      VtWord("VTCLEAREOS", ClearEos(), "Erase from the cursor to the end of the screen."),
      VtWord("VTCLEARBOS", ClearBos(), "Erase from the start of the screen to the cursor."),
      VtWord("VTCLEARSCREEN", ClearScreen(), "Erase the whole screen. Answering this and letting the shell print it really does clear your terminal.") }

Def VsStatus() As List Of VtWord
    { VtWord("VTREQUESTSTATUS", RequestStatus(), "Ask the terminal for its status. The reply arrives as input, not as this word's answer."),
      VtWord("VTREQUESTCURSORPOS", RequestCursorPos(), "Ask the terminal where the cursor is."),
      VtWord("VTREQUESTTERMINALTYPE", RequestTerminalType(), "Ask the terminal what it is."),
      VtWord("VTRESETTERMINAL", ResetTerminal(), "Reset the terminal to its power-up state.") }

Def VsTests() As List Of VtWord
    { VtWord("VTSCREENALIGNMENTTEST", ScreenAlignmentTest(), "Fill the screen with E, the classic alignment pattern."),
      VtWord("VTTESTPOWERUP", TestPowerUp(), "Run the power-up confidence test."),
      VtWord("VTTESTLOOPBACK", TestLoopback(), "Run the loopback confidence test."),
      VtWord("VTTESTPOWERUPREPEAT", TestPowerUpRepeat(), "Run the power-up test, repeating."),
      VtWord("VTTESTLOOPBACKREPEAT", TestLoopbackRepeat(), "Run the loopback test, repeating."),
      VtWord("VTLEDSOFF", LedsOff(), "Turn every keyboard LED off."),
      VtWord("VTLED1ON", Led1On(), "Turn keyboard LED 1 on."),
      VtWord("VTLED2ON", Led2On(), "Turn keyboard LED 2 on."),
      VtWord("VTLED3ON", Led3On(), "Turn keyboard LED 3 on."),
      VtWord("VTLED4ON", Led4On(), "Turn keyboard LED 4 on.") }

Def VsVt52() As List Of VtWord
    { VtWord("VTVT52GRAPHICS", Vt52SetGraphics(), "VT52 mode: use the graphics character set."),
      VtWord("VTVT52NOGRAPHICS", Vt52ResetGraphics(), "VT52 mode: back to the ordinary character set."),
      VtWord("VTVT52UP", Vt52CursorUp(), "VT52 mode: cursor up one line."),
      VtWord("VTVT52DOWN", Vt52CursorDown(), "VT52 mode: cursor down one line."),
      VtWord("VTVT52RIGHT", Vt52CursorRight(), "VT52 mode: cursor right one column."),
      VtWord("VTVT52LEFT", Vt52CursorLeft(), "VT52 mode: cursor left one column."),
      VtWord("VTVT52HOME", Vt52CursorHome(), "VT52 mode: cursor to the top left."),
      VtWord("VTVT52REVERSEINDEX", Vt52ReverseIndex(), "VT52 mode: up one line, scrolling if at the top."),
      VtWord("VTVT52CLEAREOL", Vt52ClearEol(), "VT52 mode: erase to the end of the line."),
      VtWord("VTVT52CLEAREOS", Vt52ClearEos(), "VT52 mode: erase to the end of the screen."),
      VtWord("VTVT52IDENT", Vt52Ident(), "VT52 mode: ask the terminal to identify itself."),
      VtWord("VTVT52IDENTRESPONSE", Vt52IdentResponse(), "VT52 mode: the reply a VT52 sends to that question.") }

Def VsAllConsts() As List Of VtWord
    Concat(Concat(Concat(VsBuilding(), VsModes()), Concat(VsKeypad(), VsCharsets())),
           Concat(Concat(VsAttrs(), VsCursor()), Concat(Concat(VsTabs(), VsLines()),
                  Concat(Concat(VsErasing(), VsStatus()), Concat(VsTests(), VsVt52())))))

Rem ---- the words that take numbers --------------------------------------

Rem A VT100 counts rows and columns from 1, and a count of lines to move
Rem is a whole number of 1 or more. RckWholeArg refuses negatives and
Rem fractions; 0 is refused here, because a sequence with a 0 in it is
Rem accepted by the terminal and does something other than what the
Rem caller meant.

Def VsSeedMoves(st As RckState) As RckState
    Let s1 = VsRegMove(st, "VTCURSORUP", CursorUp, "Move the cursor up n lines.")
    Let s2 = VsRegMove(s1, "VTCURSORDOWN", CursorDown, "Move the cursor down n lines.")
    Let s3 = VsRegMove(s2, "VTCURSORRIGHT", CursorRight, "Move the cursor right n columns.")
    VsRegMove(s3, "VTCURSORLEFT", CursorLeft, "Move the cursor left n columns.")

Def VsRegMove(st As RckState, name As String, f As [ Number -- String ], desc As String) As RckState
    RckReg(st, name, 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => VsMoveOf(name, vs, f)), "( n -- str )", desc)

Def VsMoveOf(word As String, vs As List Of Cell, f As [ Number -- String ]) As RckWork
    Select Case RckWholeArg(word, First(vs))
        Case Ok(n)
            VsMoveBy(word, n, f)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Def VsMoveBy(word As String, n As Number, f As [ Number -- String ]) As RckWork
    If n < 1 Then
        RckWorkBad(word & ": THE COUNT MUST BE 1 OR MORE")
    Else
        RckVals({ CutStr(Call(f, n)) })

Def VsSeedPlaces(st As RckState) As RckState
    Let s1 = VsRegPlace(st, "VTCURSORPOS", CursorPos, "The sequence that puts the cursor at row, col. Both count from 1.")
    Let s2 = VsRegPlace(s1, "VTHVPOS", HvPos, "The same position, by the horizontal-and-vertical sequence rather than the cursor one.")
    Let s3 = VsRegPlace(s2, "VTVT52POS", Vt52CursorPos, "VT52 mode: put the cursor at row, col.")
    VsRegPlace(s3, "VTSCROLLREGION", SetScrollRegion, "Confine scrolling to the lines from top to bottom. Both count from 1, and top must be above bottom.")

Def VsRegPlace(st As RckState, name As String, f As [ Number Number -- String ], desc As String) As RckState
    RckReg(st, name, 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => VsPlaceOf(name, vs, f)), "( a b -- str )", desc)

Def VsPlaceOf(word As String, vs As List Of Cell, f As [ Number Number -- String ]) As RckWork
    Select Case Pair(RckWholeArg(word, Nth(vs, 1)), RckWholeArg(word, Nth(vs, 2)))
        Case Pair(Ok(a), Ok(b))
            VsPlaceAt(word, a, b, f)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " needs two whole numbers")

Rem The ordering check is only asked of the scrolling region, because it
Rem is the only one of the four where the two numbers are a RANGE rather
Rem than a point, and a region whose top is below its bottom is a typo
Rem the terminal would act on.
Def VsPlaceAt(word As String, a As Number, b As Number, f As [ Number Number -- String ]) As RckWork
    If a < 1 Or b < 1 Then
        RckWorkBad(word & ": ROWS AND COLUMNS COUNT FROM 1")
    Else
        VsPlaceOrdered(word, a, b, f)

Def VsPlaceOrdered(word As String, a As Number, b As Number, f As [ Number Number -- String ]) As RckWork
    If word = "VTSCROLLREGION" And a >= b Then
        RckWorkBad("VTSCROLLREGION: THE TOP LINE MUST BE ABOVE THE BOTTOM ONE")
    Else
        RckVals({ CutStr(Call(f, a, b)) })

Rem ---- reading a keystroke back -----------------------------------------

Rem The other direction, and the only word here that takes a string. Per
Rem R4.16 there is no KEY cell, and a Vt100Key has eleven cases — so it
Rem answers the key's NAME, which is what every caller of it wants: the
Rem arrows and PF keys by name, a keypad key as its own character, and
Rem anything unrecognised as "unknown".
Def VsSeedEval(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "VTEVALKEY", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => VsEvalOf(vs)), "( raw -- str )", "What escape sequence raw is, as a name: \"up\", \"down\", \"left\", \"right\", \"pf1\" to \"pf4\", \"enter\", the character itself for a keypad key, or \"unknown\". Both the application-mode and normal-mode forms are recognised.")

Def VsEvalOf(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckTextArg("VTEVALKEY", First(vs))
        Case Ok(raw)
            RckVals({ CutStr(VsKeyName(EvalKey(raw))) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("VTEVALKEY did not answer")

Def VsKeyName(k As Vt100Key) As String
    Select Case k
        Case KeyUp
            "up"
        Case KeyDown
            "down"
        Case KeyLeft
            "left"
        Case KeyRight
            "right"
        Case KeyPF1
            "pf1"
        Case KeyPF2
            "pf2"
        Case KeyPF3
            "pf3"
        Case KeyPF4
            "pf4"
        Case KeyEnter
            "enter"
        Case KeyPad(ch)
            ch
        Case Else
            "unknown"

Rem ---- the seed ----------------------------------------------------------

Def RckSeedVt100(st As RckState) As RckState
    Let s1 = VsRegAll(RckSeeding(st, "seed-vt100"), VsAllConsts())
    Let s2 = VsSeedMoves(s1)
    Let s3 = VsSeedPlaces(s2)
    VsSeedEval(s3)
